            <div xml:id="d707">

                <pb facs="0811" n="699" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg699"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13304: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Hankow</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Josselyn</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-12-04T18:00:00+08:00">December 4, 1935—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 4—11:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>65. My December 3, 4 p.m.<anchor n="22" xml:id="d707-f1"/> Red movements at
                    present uncertain. Chinese military Hankow state main force Reds are in central
                    Hunan between Hsienhua and Supu and so far as they know are still moving
                    westward. On the other hand telegram from Chihkiang received by Catholic Mission
                    today states Reds retreating from that point. News received today from Changsha
                    is more reassuring and former nervousness reported not now apparent.</p>
                <p>2. All missionaries in western Hunan (referred to in my November 30, 4 p.m.,
                    paragraph 2) have now been heard from.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Department, Peiping and Nanking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Josselyn</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
